\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-12x-9 & =0 \\\\ -3({x}^{2}+4x+3) & =0 \\\\ -3(x+3)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-3,-1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-(-3)^{3}-6(-3)^{2}-9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}-9(-1)+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global minimum is $3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061690/assets/2eca6b83a3f99ca87a70e381490e99c98e80a535cd3051a4cdd0740f82de577b.png)" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341685 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341686 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341687 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341688 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341689 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1041 "selection" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341686 13194139535285 true] [17592186341684 1110 #uuid "6461a09e-135d-5733-8160-60ec782fc040" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341685 1034 "9" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535285 true] [17592186341685 1111 #uuid "d874ba62-7ea7-5fe3-9ad7-237c77d3d2ae" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341686 1034 "7" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341686 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535285 true] [17592186341686 1111 #uuid "f32d00c1-e054-583d-95bc-7220e2293115" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341687 1034 "6" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535285 true] [17592186341687 1111 #uuid "c53dc68a-ad5b-53c4-b840-eeb818a40bbe" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341688 1034 "4" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535285 true] [17592186341688 1111 #uuid "9459ae53-92cb-536c-88e7-3104b0184d00" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341689 1034 "5" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535285 true] [17592186341689 1111 #uuid "6fdfb5d2-6557-59f7-bcd3-5f9c49cf1d17" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341690 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341691 13194139535285 true] [17592186341690 1089 "q-344460" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341690 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535285 true] [17592186341690 1100 "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535285 true] [17592186341690 1113 #uuid "55363c3c-02e7-50b7-9197-4efdcda472d7" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341690 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[0,2],$ the critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=0,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061690/assets/463d11ca52d41491a6184a1d7d39692af43e53aa0fd470324a8f8e999c842811.png)" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341692 13194139535285 true] [17592186341691 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535285 true] [17592186341691 1041 "field-1" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341691 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341692 13194139535285 true] [17592186341691 1110 #uuid "0ecafe37-e989-54bd-abcf-774b5973feda" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341692 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535285 true] [17592186341692 1111 #uuid "aa8182fb-7429-5094-99af-5c3a2db42d89" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341693 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341694 13194139535285 true] [17592186341693 1089 "q-79341" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341693 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535285 true] [17592186341693 1100 "For the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{9}{e}$\n- The global maximum value is $4$\n- The global minimum value is $e$" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341693 1113 #uuid "234b945a-d7d5-5235-a923-8856a2131a80" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341693 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2(x-2){e}^{x}+(x-2)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x}[2+x-2] \\\\  & =x(x-2){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nx(x-2){e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1-2)^{2}{e}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{e}≈3.31 \\\\ f(0) & =(0-2)^{2}{e}^{0}=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1-2)^{2}{e}^{1}=e≈2.72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061690/assets/adeb7eb92f270a216f30cd0466e91d0363c5a50b17053316e6e9f44d0dfaaf23.png)" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341695 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341696 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341697 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341698 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341699 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1041 "selection" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341695 13194139535285 true] [17592186341694 1110 #uuid "c84366d3-19cf-5173-8fa9-d3384145cc07" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341695 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535285 true] [17592186341695 1111 #uuid "71e24774-12a1-53e2-bc02-7f3feafd73b5" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341696 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341696 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535285 true] [17592186341696 1111 #uuid "e7d21d9e-6411-5640-8b0a-bc10e5b573ff" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341697 1034 "I only" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341697 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535285 true] [17592186341697 1111 #uuid "aee9966f-818a-59bc-8bd9-4e0a475857f5" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341698 1034 "III only" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535285 true] [17592186341698 1111 #uuid "4f08028a-09db-5b71-a04e-5630aad6071c" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341699 1034 "II only" 13194139535285 true] [17592186341699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535285 true] [17592186341